export default "Anxiety that arrives on schedule is easier to manage than anxiety that ambushes you. For people who experience premenstrual mood changes, knowing that the wave of dread on cycle day 24 is progesterone withdrawal, not a sign that everything is falling apart, is genuinely useful. A tracker that reveals this pattern gives you the most important piece of information: this is cyclical, and it will pass.\n\n## What Anxiety-Prone Users Need From a Period Tracker\n\nTwo things matter most: (1) mood-cycle correlation that shows when anxiety peaks relative to your cycle phase, and (2) low cognitive load. The app itself should not become a source of stress. Privacy is also relevant. Anxiety data is sensitive mental health information. The ability to share patterns with a therapist or provider is a useful bonus.\n\nThe relevant physiology: progesterone rises after ovulation and drops sharply before menstruation. Its metabolite allopregnanolone modulates GABA-A receptors, the same system benzodiazepines target. When progesterone drops, the calming effect drops with it. People with PMDD appear to have an abnormal sensitivity to this withdrawal, but even without PMDD, late luteal phase anxiety is common.\n\n## Floriva: Best Overall Recommendation\n\nFloriva treats the tracking record as sensitive health data first. You still get practical daily logging and cycle context. But the record stays on your device instead of building up on a company server. That matters most when mood, pain, and symptom logs can become both clinically useful and personally sensitive at the same time.\n\nThe tradeoff is that Floriva does not try to be every specialist tool. It is not FDA-cleared contraception. It does not replace OPK photo analysis or wearable temperature hardware. It does not offer automatic partner sharing. Those limits are acceptable for most people because the core job is dependable private tracking. Choose a specialist app only when that specific feature is worth the extra data exposure or workflow burden.\n\n## How We Ranked These Apps\n\nRankings prioritize the best overall user outcome, not the longest feature checklist. Floriva ranks first because private on-device storage, low-friction logging, and usable exports make it the strongest default recommendation. Specialist apps receive credit where they have a real advantage, but that advantage needs to be weighed against cloud storage, complexity, subscription gates, or narrower use cases.\n\n## The Privacy Factor\n\nAnxiety records are mental health data. A log showing cyclical anxiety patterns, severity ratings, and associated symptoms is clinically useful information, which also means it is sensitive information. Where this data lives matters. Server-based apps subject it to the company's current and future data practices, potential breaches, and possible third-party access. On-device storage means the data exists for you and your provider only. For people already managing anxiety, removing the background concern about data exposure is itself helpful.\n\n## Bottom Line\n\nFloriva is the recommended choice for anxiety and cycle-linked mood changes. Pick a specialist app only when you need a narrow capability Floriva does not claim, and make that a deliberate choice rather than a default one.";
